        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe exception class and the exception handler are both clean and idiomatic \u2014 `FutureRegistrationDateException` mirrors `DailyRegistrationLimitException` exactly (same package, header, `LocalDate` constructor, web-layer mapping note), and the new handler is a faithful copy of the sibling handlers with the correct 400 status and message constant. No concerns there.\n\nThe one structural issue is in `ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner`:\n\n**Finding: the future-date validation breaks the established per-rule `assert*` idiom and entangles validation with date-defaulting.**\n\nThis codebase expresses every create-time business rule as a dedicated, named private helper that guards and throws its domain exception:\n- `assertIdentityIsUnique(owner)` \u2192 `DuplicateIdentityException`\n- `assertCityHasCapacity(owner)` \u2192 `CityAtCapacityException`\n- `assertDailyRegistrationLimit(owner)` \u2192 `DailyRegistrationLimitException`\n\n`saveOwner` then reads as a flat sequence of those asserts. The new rule \u2014 \"reject a `registrationDate` later than today,\" which throws a domain exception mapped to an HTTP error \u2014 is the exact same category of rule, but it's been inlined as an `else if` bolted onto the null-defaulting block (lines 285\u2013290). That does two things a maintainer wouldn't expect here:\n- It diverges from the strong, uniform `assert*`-helper idiom, so this one rule is harder to find and sits at a different \"altitude\" than its three siblings.\n- It fuses a *validation* concern onto a *defaulting* concern. The two blocks happen to be mutually exclusive (null vs non-null), which makes the `else if` look tidy, but it couples unrelated responsibilities.\n\nSuggested change \u2014 extract a helper matching the existing three and call it after defaulting, which also lets the `else if` collapse (a defaulted `now()` is never in the future, so the check is a harmless no-op for the null case):\n\n```java\nif (owner.getRegistrationDate() == null) {\n    owner.setRegistrationDate(LocalDate.now());\n}\nassertRegistrationDateNotInFuture(owner);\nowner.setRegistrationDate(rollToBusinessDay(owner.getRegistrationDate()));\n```\n\n```java\nprivate void assertRegistrationDateNotInFuture(Owner owner) {\n    LocalDate registrationDate = owner.getRegistrationDate();\n    if (registrationDate != null && registrationDate.isAfter(LocalDate.now())) {\n        throw new FutureRegistrationDateException(registrationDate);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis keeps the validation in the right unit, restores consistency with the surrounding rules, and keeps `saveOwner` reading as a uniform list of asserts."
      }
